## Problem and quantifiers

\[
D(n)=\min\{\operatorname{diam}(A):A\subset\mathbb R^2,\ |A|=n,\ |x-y|\ge1\}.
\]

Erdős Problem #99 asks whether there is \(n_0\) such that for every \(n\ge n_0\),
**every** global minimizer contains a unit equilateral triangle.

A proof that some minimizer contains a triangle is insufficient. A local or
near-optimal triangle-free packing is not a disproof. A disproof requires
triangle-free **global minimizers** for arbitrarily large cardinalities.

## Authoritative Target A endpoint

For the plateau-terminalized Schur response,

\[
\zeta=\dim\ker S=|C^\partial|-\operatorname{rank}S,
\qquad
s=\Delta_\Lambda-d_{\rm int}.
\]

A hypothetical survivor has

\[
\boxed{g=\Delta_\Lambda-d_{\rm sc}=s-\zeta=\rho^\dagger-\Phi(v)\ge1.}
\]

Target A closes exactly if

\[
\boxed{\zeta\ge s,}
\]

or equivalently

\[
\boxed{\operatorname{rank}S
\le d_{\rm int}+|C^\partial|-\Delta_\Lambda.}
\]

The Schur response is an exact planar strict gammoid: its rank is a maximum
vertex-disjoint alternating linkage and a minimum directed vertex separator. Planarity
alone does not force the required rank loss.

## Critical source-Hall correction

Let the genuine source be \(A\subset H=\operatorname{conv}A\), \(|A|=N\), and put

\[
\widehat p_H=\alpha_1(H)-N.
\]

The exact fixed-container theorem gives, for **every** finite unit-separated
\(Q\subset H\),

\[
\boxed{|Q|-|N_A(Q)|\le\widehat p_H.}
\]

Therefore the formerly proposed target

\[
|Q|-|N_A(Q)|\ge\widehat p_H+1
\]

inside the same hull is **impossible**, not an open lemma. Do not attempt to prove it.
The same correction applies after maximal triangle-free extension \(B\): if
\(\tau=\alpha_1(H)-|B|\), no unit-separated \(Q\subset H\) can have
\(|Q|-|N_B(Q)|>\tau\).

A source replacement can close Target A only when it carries extra information not
contained in the same-hull Hall number—for example:

1. an \(N+1\)-point unit-separated competitor inside a set of diameter **strictly
   below** \(D(N)\); or
2. a direct transport of source/transition data into \(s\) independent Schur-kernel
   directions / alternating nonlinkages.

This correction is binding.

## Exact source-capacity flag and its limitation

For the maximal triangle-free extension \(B\subset H\), choose a capacity endpoint
\(X\subset H\) with \(|X|=\alpha_1(H)=|B|+\tau\). The fixed-basis fundamental-circuit
construction gives global optima

\[
P_E=(B\setminus W_E)\cup U_E,
\qquad
|P_E|=|B|+|E|,
\qquad
\operatorname{diam}P_E=D.
\]

The exact source-Hall profile is

\[
N_A(P_E)=A,
\qquad
|P_E|-|N_A(P_E)|=k+|E|,
\]

where \(|B|=N+k\). Its residual capacity slack is \(\tau-|E|\), and

\[
\boxed{(k+|E|)+(\tau-|E|)=\widehat p_H.}
\]

Thus the exchange flag consumes the available source slack exactly and reaches, but
can never exceed, \(\widehat p_H\). Do not try to obtain the missing integer merely
by pushing the fixed-hull flag farther.

If all flag members are triangle-free, the chain reaches a triangle-free capacity
endpoint. Otherwise let \(P_j\) be the first triangle-producing member. Every new
triangle meets \(\Delta X_j\), and the transition is an exact net-+1 Hall-tight
exchange.

## First-transition geometry now proved

The active Target A module contains two rigorous perturbation results.

1. **One-point semicircle/cage dichotomy.** A non-diametral inserted point whose unit
   contact directions lie in a closed semicircle can be moved slightly so that all
   newly created triangles disappear while feasibility and diameter are preserved.
   If the directions are not contained in a closed semicircle, the point is locally
   isolated in the one-point feasible slice.

2. **Simultaneous fixed-container locked-triangle alternative.** For a unit-separated
   configuration in a convex hull, either one expansive-cone velocity simultaneously
   breaks every unit triangle, or there exists a unit triangle all three of whose edge
   derivatives vanish for every fixed-container infinitesimally feasible velocity.
   Applied to the first bad flag member, the first alternative gives a triangle-free
   global optimum at the same new cardinality.

The locked branch is real. Proposition 139.1 gives an exact two-leaf tree-circuit
family for which either leaf swap creates an infinitesimally pinned unit triangle.
Therefore leaf ordering and one-point perturbation alone cannot close Target A.

**Do not claim that one locked triangle gives one Schur null direction.** That
transport theorem is still missing.

## Homothetic strain and Kuperberg gap concentration

For a free triangular rhombic patch

\[
P_L=(1+\varepsilon_L)R_L,
\]

Section 137 proves:

\[
L\varepsilon_L\ge\eta>0
\quad\Longrightarrow\quad
\alpha_1(\operatorname{conv}P_L)\ge|P_L|+1
\]

for all sufficiently large \(L\). In particular this covers every fixed
\(\varepsilon_L=\alpha/L\), \(\alpha>0\). The proof concentrates the accumulated
strain into a fixed positive linear gap and inserts one finite
Kuperberg–Kuperberg–Kuperberg witness.

This theorem is **free-patch only**. An embedded monomer domain may have exterior
blockers; a successful Target A application needs an exterior-protected concentration
or another global transport argument.

The post-v1.7 addendum also proves the vanishing-gap complexity theorem. If
\(\kappa_*(d)\) is the smallest number of deleted old centers in any positive-gain
finite rearrangement of any triangular-lattice gap packing of width \(d\), then

\[
\boxed{\kappa_*(d)\to\infty\qquad(d\downarrow0).}
\]

Kuperberg proves finiteness for every fixed positive gap, but no explicit growth rate
is presently established. Do not assume \(O(d^{-c})\), \(\Theta(d^{-c})\), or any
other rate.

## Other binding source facts

Use, when relevant:

- strict-rattler product-germ theorem: finite-order local KKT/stress cannot see strict
  rattler coordinates;
- exact inner-parallel capacity theorem;
- same-cardinality rattler-preserving source saturation;
- capacity-shadow injection \(A\to X_{\max}\) with displacement \(<1\), same-cardinality
  global optimum partner, and hull Hausdorff distance \(<1\);
- finite-chord stress inequality;
- only collinear root paths can support codimension-one affine triangle walls;
- monotone activation normal form, persistent unit-activation transfer, activation-edge
  forest, and independence of its rigidity rows.

These are exact tools, not a license to identify representatives. In particular,
replacing or extending the source may change its contact graph and requires rebuilding
any scaffold whose construction depended on the old representative.

## Highest-value next attacks

Prioritize one of these exact bridges:

1. **Locked-triangle → Schur transport.** Use global first-transition/source data—not
   local cage geometry alone—to turn locked triangle constraints into at least
   \(s=\Delta_\Lambda-d_{\rm int}\) independent failures of the boundary alternating
   linkage.

2. **Exterior-compatible strain concentration.** Upgrade the free-rhombus add-one
   theorem to a coherent monomer domain with the actual exterior source/scaffold
   blockers and a strict diameter certificate.

3. **Capacity-shadow transport.** Push the bounded-displacement same-cardinality
   source shadow through deletion/plateau terminalization and prove that its boundary
   displacement lands in an \(s\)-dimensional kernel of \(S\).

4. **Activation-rank compensation.** Show that the large independent activation-edge
   forest forced by a super-boundary rattler source requires compensating dependence
   in the boundary Schur response.

5. **Vanishing-gap quantitative theory.** Derive an explicit rate or constructive
   upper/lower bound for Kuperberg witness complexity only if it can be connected to
   the scale and protection available in the actual monomer scaffold.

Regression-test every proposed theorem against the exact two-leaf cage, strained
monomer patch, mixed monomer interface, cyclic Hall circuit, regular-shell, long
rooted-chain, and overlapping-circuit examples already stored in the repository.

## Status discipline

Do not call Target A solved until the exact Schur inequality is proved or a genuine
source replacement gives a strictly smaller-diameter global competitor. Do not call a
local, asymptotic, or finite regression a global counterexample.

**LEVEL I STATUS: STILL OPEN. TARGET A REMAINS UNRESOLVED.**
